
 viewcheckusage - Report dscheck delayed-mode command processing history.

 Queries dscheck history records in PostgreSQL database 'rdadb' and
 produces a check usage view/report grouped and filtered by the options
 you choose.

 USAGE:
   viewcheckusage [-C] ColumnNames [-O OrderColumnNames]
                  <one or more condition options>
                  [output and miscellaneous options]
                  [> OutputFile] [| lp -d PrinterName]

 BASIC RULES:

   1. -C ColumnNames is required (see COLUMN OPTIONS below).
      The '-C' flag may be omitted if ColumnNames is the first argument.

   2. At least one condition option must be given:
        -a -b -B -c -d -D -h -i -m -n -q -r -s -S -t -T -y

   3. Negation: for any condition option except -a, prepend '!' between
      the flag and value to invert the filter, e.g. '-c ! dsarch'.

   4. Wildcards: condition options that take strings (-c, -n, -s, -S, -t)
      accept SQL wildcards '%' (any chars) and '_' (one char).

   5. By default the output is paginated with a header, footer, and
      column titles. Use -w for plain output (no header/totals).


 COLUMN OPTIONS:

   -C ColumnNames
        A string of one or more column letters listed below. The display
        order of columns matches their order in the string. At least one
        group column or summary column is required.

   -O OrderColumnNames
        Sort the output. Each letter must also appear in -C.
        UPPERCASE = ascending, lowercase = descending.

   Column letters:

     Group Columns:
       A* - ARGV        - command argument string
       C* - COMMAND     - command name (e.g. 'dsarch')
       D* - DATE        - check date, YYYY-MM-DD (e.g. 2004-04-25)
       E* - ERRMSG      - error message if the check failed
       G* - TRIED       - number of tries (always 1 except for 'dsarch')
       H  - HOSTNAME    - hosts the check can/cannot run on
       I* - CHKIDX      - check index
       K* - TIME        - check time, HH:MM:SS
       L* - BATCHID     - batch id (if a batch job)
       M* - MONTH       - YYYY-MM (e.g. 2004-04)
       N* - AN          - action name (e.g. BR for dsrqst)
       P* - DATAPROC    - amount of data processed
       Q* - QUETIME     - seconds spent in queue (batch jobs)
       R  - RUNTIME     - total run time in seconds (queue time included)
       S  - SPECIALIST  - specialist who owns the check
       T* - DATASET     - format dsnnn.n (e.g. d540001)
       W* - STATUS      - D=Done, F=Finished, E=Exit, I=Interrupted
       Y* - YEAR        - YYYY

       (*) = column may be displayed with zero usages when -z is used.

     Summary Columns:
       B - MBYTESDATA  - data size (MB) by the group
       F - FILECOUNT   - number of files to be processed by the group
       J - #FILEPROC   - number of unique files processed by the group
       U - HREXEC      - execution hours by the group
       V - HRQUEUE     - queue hours on batch nodes by the group
       Z - #CHECKS     - number of checks by the group

     Index Column:
       X - INDEX       - 1-based row index (use as the first column)


 CONDITION OPTIONS:

   -a   All check usage history.
   -A RowLimit                       Limit the number of rows returned.
   -b BatchIDs                       Checks with these batch IDs.
   -B MinBatchID [MaxBatchID]        Checks with batch IDs in this range.
   -c CommandNames                   Checks with these command names.
   -d DateList                       Checks on these dates (YYYY-MM-DD).
   -D StartDate [EndDate]            Checks between two dates.
   -h HostNames                      Checks on these host names.
   -i CheckIndices                   Checks with these indices.
   -m MonthList                      Checks in these months (YYYY-MM).
   -n ActionNames                    Checks with these action names.
   -q MinSec [MaxSec]                Checks queued for this many seconds.
   -r MinSec [MaxSec]                Checks run for this many seconds
                                     (queue time included).
   -s CheckStatusList                Checks ending with these statuses.
   -S SpecialistLoginNames           Specialists who own the checks.
   -t DatasetList                    Checks of these datasets.
   -T MinDataset [MaxDataset]        Checks of datasets in this ID range,
                                     e.g. '-T d540000 d550009'.
   -y YearList                       Checks in these years (YYYY).


 OUTPUT AND MISCELLANEOUS OPTIONS:

   -H Title           Replace the default report title.
   -L Delimiter       Use Delimiter between columns instead of spaces.
   -U SizeUnit        Show sizes in unit: B, K, M, or G.
   -w                 Simple format: no header, no totals.
   -z                 Include zero usages for the specified fields.
   > OutputFile       Redirect output to a file.
   | lp -d Printer    Send output to the named printer (e.g. lj100).


 EXAMPLES:

   View monthly check usage in 2012 with columns INDEX, COMMAND, MONTH,
   #CHECKS, MBYTESDATA; sort by MONTH ascending and MBYTESDATA descending:

      viewcheckusage XCMZB -y 2012 -O Mb

   Same, restricted to commands starting with 'ds':

      viewcheckusage XCMZB -y 2012 -c ds% -O Mb

   Same, excluding 'dsarch':

      viewcheckusage XCMZB -y 2012 -c ! dsarch -O Mb

   Save to a file:

      viewcheckusage XCMZB -y 2012 -O Mb > checkusage.out
